E=[
(3,1894,"Falls","Ice slabs from a Portland tornado","A tornado is reported from Portland, Oregon, and fragments of ice, three to four inches square and about an inch thick, fall from the sky. Fort cites the Monthly Weather Review as evidence for flat ice fields overhead.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Monthly Weather Review, July 1894","a tornado, of June 3, 1894, was reported. Fragments of ice fell from the sky"),
(6,1890,"Falls","Limestone pebbles fall with hail","Limestone pebbles fall at Pel-et-Der, France, with hail. Fort argues they could not have been carried from the nearest limestone, since June hail cannot be traced to a distant source.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing La Nature, 1890-2-127","June 6, 1890, of limestone pebbles"),
(7,1779,"Sky","Luminous globes over Boulogne","An earthquake at Boulogne, France comes with a prodigious number of luminous bodies, or globes, in the air. Fort lists it among earthquakes accompanied by lights in the sky.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Sestier, La Foudre, 1-169","June 7, 1779"),
(8,1901,"Falls","Glue falls at Sart","About 2 p.m., a glue-like substance falls at Sart, Belgium. The meteorologist M. Michael says he saw it falling, and Ciel et Terre published his account.","NL","New Lands, citing Ciel et Terre, 22-198","about 2 P. M., June 8, 1901, a glue-like substance fell at Sart"),
(9,1874,"Falls","Hail with carbonate of soda","Hail falls at Elizabeth, New Jersey containing a substance that Prof. Leeds of Stevens Institute says is carbonate of soda. Fort cites Scientific American.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Scientific American, 30-262","at Elizabeth, N.J., June 9, 1874, fell hail"),
(10,1909,"Sea","A wheel of light in the Straits of Malacca","At 3 a.m., Captain Gabe of the steamship Bintang sees a vast revolving wheel of light lying flat on the water, with long arms issuing from a centre. Fort cites the Danish Meteorological Institute.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Danish Meteorological Institute report","At 3 A.M., June 10, 1909, while sailing through the Straits of Malacca"),
(11,1855,"Cosmos","A naked-eye body crosses the Sun","Ritter and Schmidt see a dark body so large it is visible without telescopes crossing the Sun. Fort adds it to his list of dark bodies crossing the Sun.","NL","New Lands, citing Observatory, 3-137","June 11, 1855-a dark body of such size that it was seen, without telescopes"),
(12,1858,"Falls","A shower of aerolites on Birmingham","Dr. C. Mansfield Ingleby writes to the Birmingham Daily Post that on Saturday morning a storm brought a shower of aerolites to Birmingham, many hundreds of thousands, he thought. Fort sets it before the 1868 stone falls.","NL","New Lands, citing Birmingham Daily Post, June 14, 1858","During the storm on Saturday (12th) morning, Birmingham was visited by a shower of aerolites"),
(13,1889,"Falls","Fishes fall in Holland","L'Astronomie lists a fall of fishes in Holland, followed by ants at Strasbourg on Aug. 1 and little toads in Savoy on Aug. 2 the same year.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing L'Astronomie, 1889-353","Fall of fishes, June 13, 1889, in Holland"),
(14,1880,"Falls","Red, blue and gray hail in Russia","According to Prof. Schwedoff, red hailstones, blue hailstones and gray hailstones fall in Russia. Fort cites La Nature for it, and adds a Venezuelan report of 1886.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing La Nature, 1885-2-351","there fell, in Russia, June 14, 1880, red hailstones"),
(15,1829,"Falls","Ice block at Cazorta","A block of ice weighing four and a half pounds falls at Cazorta, Spain. Flammarion lists it in The Atmosphere with other large ice falls, such as an 11-pound block at Cette.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Flammarion, The Atmosphere, p. 34","Cazorta, Spain, June 15, 1829"),
(16,1882,"Falls","Hailstones with something inside at Dubuque","In a storm at Dubuque, Iowa, hailstones and pieces of ice fall, and the foreman of the Novelty Iron Works says two large hailstones he melted held foreign matter. Fort cites the Monthly Weather Review.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Monthly Weather Review, June 1882","Storm at Dubuque, Iowa, June 16, 1882"),
(17,1777,"Cosmos","Dark spheres cross the Sun","Messier reports a prodigious number of dark, spherical bodies crossing the Sun. Fort sets it beside similar mass-transit reports by other observers.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Arago, OEuvres, 9-38","prodigious number of dark, spherical bodies reported by Messier, June 17, 1777"),
(18,1839,"Falls","Darkness and ice at Brussels","A profound darkness comes over Brussels, and flat pieces of ice an inch long fall. Fort cites Flammarion's The Atmosphere as a case of the Sun eclipsed by ice fields.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Flammarion, The Atmosphere, p. 394","a profound darkness came upon the city of Brussels, June 18, 1839"),
(20,1887,"Falls","The Tarbes stone","In a violent storm a small stone falls at Tarbes, France, two months before the fall of the symmetric iron object of Brixton. Fort cites Comptes Rendus.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Comptes Rendus, 1887-182","a small stone had fallen from the sky at Tarbes"),
(23,1744,"Haunts","Phantom soldiers on a mountain","According to 27 witnesses, some sworn before a magistrate, troops of aerial soldiers are seen on and over a mountain for two hours, vanishing only with darkness. Brewster called it a mirage of troops.","NL","New Lands, citing Brewster, Natural Magic, p. 125 and Clarke's Survey of the Lakes","a review of the phenomenon of June 23, 1744"),
(24,1911,"Falls","Jelly the size of peas falls at Eton","After a heavy rainfall at Eton, Bucks, the ground is covered with masses of jelly the size of peas. Nature reported it, and the jelly contained numerous organisms. Small frogs fell at Wigan the same time.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Nature, 87-10","June 24, 1911, at Eton, Bucks, England, the ground was found covered with masses of jelly"),
(25,1894,"Mysteries","Cannon-like detonations at Louvain","M. Van den Broeck at Louvain hears detonations like artillery discharges, and tabulates the intervals. He published it in Ciel et Terre. Fort asks whether they were signals from over Belgium.","NL","New Lands, citing Ciel et Terre, 16-485","Upon June 25, 1894, at Louvain, he had heard detonations like discharges of artillery"),
(26,1819,"Cosmos","Five unknown bodies cross the Sun","Gruithuisen sees five unknown bodies cross the Sun, and Pastorff sees something he takes for a comet near it. Fort lists it with other solar transits.","NL","New Lands, citing An. Sci. Disc., 1860-411","Five unknown bodies that were seen, upon June 26, 1819, crossing the sun"),
(27,1896,"Cosmos","A black object crosses the Moon","A writer to Science Gossip, looking at the Moon at one in the morning, sees a long black object sail across it from west to east in 3 or 4 seconds. Fort compares it to the 'Brooks object'.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Science Gossip","on June 27, 1896, at one o'clock in the morning, he was looking at the moon"),
(28,1908,"Sky","A great light at the eclipse","A great luminous object, or meteor, is seen at the time of the eclipse. W. F. Denning remarks 'as if to make the date of the eclipse more memorable'.","NL","New Lands, citing W. F. Denning, Observatory, 31-288","seen at the time of the eclipse of June 28, 1908"),
]
